    "42a26ff6-44ae-5dcc-b615-e6a344637997": {
        "record": {
            "bodyKind": "map",
            "body": "## Destination\n\nA teammate opens one invitation link, installs the app if needed and joins the workspace's server. When every saved address of that server stops answering, the app finds its current addresses in the workspace registry on acme.example and reconnects to the same server identity, without the owner sending a new invitation.\n\n## Decisions\n\n- The registry accepts signed workspace records only from servers holding a valid server pass; only devices holding the workspace's share secret can read them.\n- Invitation addresses are full URLs with ports, so a changed port is a changed address; overlay ranges and bracketed IPv6 addresses count as private.\n- A new device's first sign-in goes through the platform: one browser sheet for Lar sign-in, then a list of the workspaces the person belongs to.\n- Every invitation is one acme.example link that carries the whole invitation in the URL fragment, so acme.example never receives it.\n- When an owner sets a server up again, the registry keeps the server it replaced and names it, so apps can follow it once and say so.\n\n## Boundaries\n\n- Public server listings, media relay, billing changes and server backups.\n- Adding a device while the platform is down on clients other than Apple's.\n- Testing on an IPv6-only network.\n\n## Check\n\nReleased apps join, recover and sign in against the live platform and a workspace server.\n\n- A phone on cellular without the app joins through an emailed invitation link.\n- After the router's external port changes, a signed-in phone on cellular reconnects by itself within a minute.\n- A device with no saved server signs in with Lar and connects at home and on cellular.\n",
            "context": "",
            "buildPhase": null,
            "reviewRounds": []
        },
        "body": "",
        "format": "markdown",
        "author": null,
        "assignees": [],
        "createdAt": null,
